@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

#
# prima-materia — Latin alchemy, taken down from the register
#
# The instance was registered in traditions/registry-western-esoteric.ttl and is
# moved here now that a work has been read at the wording. One of the three
# works the registration names has been opened, the Turba philosophorum, and it
# has been read from the first dictum to the conclusion. The Rosarium and the
# Aurora consurgens are not opened, and the file says so at their work
# identifiers rather than letting the coverage state cover them.
#
# Why this one out of the register. It is the examinability-driven entrance of
# SPEC §14 taken literally: the corpus was picked because a reconnaissance said
# it names terms, cautions and conditions of failure, and it does. Seventy-two
# dicta produced nine cautions, five prerequisites of stated necessity, two acts
# of assay with a stated criterion, one term whose overrun is the failure, and
# four separate places where a run that produced nothing is charged to the
# operator. That last group is what the corpus is for.
#
# The three decisions of SPEC §14, taken before the first node.
#
# The contact route is documented and unchanged. This is not a Latin
# composition: the compiler wrote in Arabic or Hebrew, the speakers are
# graecised presocratics, and the editor of the translation used here says so on
# his first page and traces the parallels to the Byzantine alchemists through
# Berthelot. The transmission is therefore a witness for reception and never an
# independent one, and an agreement between this file and the Greek or Chinese
# alchemy is a route and not two witnesses.
#
# The recension and the edition, and this is the weak place of the file. What was
# read is Arthur Edward Waite's English translation of 1896, which takes the
# longer of the two Latin recensions as its text and gives the readings of the
# shorter one in footnotes. Three layers therefore stand between these nodes and
# the work: the Latin recension, Waite's English, and the optical transcription
# of his print in which it was read. The Latin of the Basel and Geneva
# collections is reachable and was not read; that is a debt of this run and not
# a property of the work. Grégoire Lacaze's critical edition of 2018 is not free
# and nobody here has it. Every node below carries the claim of Waite's English
# print and not of the Latin, and that is stated once here and not repeated.
#
# The number of citable passages. The whole body of the work was read, so the
# count of nodes is a count of what the work states and not of what a sample
# happened to show. Where the work says the same thing in several dicta the node
# cites the places together.
#
# Two things the translation is good enough for and one it is not. The nodes
# below turn on whether a condition is stated, on what is made to depend on
# what, on where a failure is assigned, and on which numbers are given. Those
# survive a translation. A node turning on a single Latin word is not written;
# the barbarous terms the work carries and nobody has explained — Corsufle,
# Mucra, Ebmich, Albar, Halsut, Gadenbe — are left as the translator left them
# and are not made to carry anything.
#

pmt:LatinAlchemy a pm:Tradition ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "Latin alchemy"@en, "Lateinische Alchemie"@de ;
    skos:definition "The western alchemical transmission, in which the stages of the work are described at once as operations and as states of the operator."@en ;
    skos:note "The double reading is not a modern imposition and not a settled fact either. It is a dispute the corpus carries in itself and the graph should record as one. The work read here is evidence on both sides in one volume: it prescribes weights, vessels, fires and day-counts for matter that can be handled, and it makes the whole outcome depend on the operator's patience, his freedom from haste, and God's willingness to show him the stone."@en ;
    skos:note "What was read: the Turba philosophorum in Waite's translation of 1896, complete — the epistle of Arisleus, all seventy-two dicta, and the conclusion of Agmon that stands as the last dictum of the shorter recension. The editorial preface and the footnote apparatus were read as apparatus and carry nothing here. Nothing of the Rosarium philosophorum and nothing of the Aurora consurgens is opened."@en ;
    skos:note "The shape of the work decides how it may be cited. It is a staged colloquy: seventy-two speeches under the names of graecised presocratics, each answered by the assembly, with the operative instruction embedded in the speech. There is no continuous procedure and no chapter apparatus beyond the numbering of the dicta, so the dictum is the smallest unit a later reader can find, and it is the unit used in every source below."@en ;
    skos:note "Why it is not an independent witness, stated at the registration and unchanged. The compiler worked in Arabic or Hebrew out of Greek material, the translation used here prints the Greek parallels alongside, and the transmission from there to the Latin collections is not in dispute. What the work can carry is the other thing SPEC §14 asks for, and it carries a great deal of it: terms in days, cautions with named consequences, and prerequisites whose omission the text says makes the work come to nothing."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Turba philosophorum; Rosarium philosophorum; Aurora consurgens" ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum, pmw:RosariumPhilosophorum, pmw:AuroraConsurgens, pmw:SummaPerfectionisMagisterii ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 9 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 39 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

#
# The works
#

pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Turba philosophorum"@en, "Turba philosophorum"@de ;
    skos:note "Read here complete in Arthur Edward Waite, The Turba Philosophorum, or Assembly of the Sages, London: George Redway 1896, which translates the longer of the two Latin recensions and gives the shorter one's readings in notes. Seventy-two dicta plus the epistle of Arisleus and the conclusion of Agmon."@en ;
    skos:note "Dating and ascription. The translator states on his first page that the work is the oldest extant Latin treatise on alchemy and was not written in Latin, and that its compiler wrote in Arabic or Hebrew. The literature puts the Arabic composition in the ninth century in Egypt and disputes when the Latin translation was made; that dispute was not settled in this run and nothing here turns on the date. The ascription of the speeches to Iximidrus, Anaxagoras, Pythagoras, Socrates, Plato, Democritus and the rest is a device of the compilation and is not treated here as an attribution; no pm:Attributing node is written on it."@en ;
    skos:note "The two recensions diverge in ways that touch the operations, not only the wording, and two of the divergences are recorded in the holding. At Dictum 69 the longer recension says of the redness hidden in the whiteness that it does not behove the operator to extract it and the shorter says that it does — two opposite instructions at the same place, carried as pmc:TurbaRubedoExtrahenda. At Dictum 16 the washing is twenty-one days with dew and rain, ten with salt and ten with fresh water in the longer recension, and twenty-nine, twenty and ten in the shorter. A run that wants to decide either has to go to the Latin."@en ;
    skos:note "The Latin is reachable and was not read. The work stands in the Artis auriferae collections of 1572, 1593 and 1610 and in Manget's Bibliotheca chemica curiosa of 1702, several of them digitised and freely readable; the 1610 Basel print carries the Turba twice, in both recensions, which makes it the cheapest place to settle the two divergences above. That the Latin was not read is a debt of this run."@en .

pmw:RosariumPhilosophorum a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Rosarium philosophorum"@en, "Rosarium philosophorum"@de ;
    skos:note "Named here as a work of the corpus and unopened. A bibliographic warning belongs with the identifier, because the title covers two different works that stand next to each other in the same collections and share passages verbatim: the late medieval florilegium first printed with its woodcuts at Frankfurt in 1550 and reprinted without them in Artis auriferae II, and the treatise ascribed to Arnald of Villanova as Thesaurus thesaurorum seu Rosarium philosophorum, which is divided into numbered capitula. A run that opens this work must say which of the two it read, and the chapter numbers that circulate in the literature belong to the second."@en ;
    skos:note "Why it is the next place to go. A reconnaissance reports that the florilegium cites the assay catalogue of the Summa perfectionis by name as Geber's book of tests, and prints the formula that the work is to be repeated if the fire test is not passed. If that is at the wording, the holding gains the link between an allegorical florilegium and an operative assay canon, which is the question pmc:TheTestOnTheIngredient now carries."@en .

pmw:AuroraConsurgens a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Aurora consurgens"@en, "Aurora consurgens"@de ;
    skos:note "Named here as a work of the corpus and unopened, with the same kind of warning as the Rosarium. The work has two parts of entirely different character and availability: the seven parables ascribed to Thomas Aquinas, which never entered the printed collections and are edited by Marie-Louise von Franz in 1957, and a second, operative tract printed in Artis auriferae I under the title Aurora consurgens quae dicitur Aurea hora. The parables are allegory throughout; a reconnaissance reports that it is the second part that carries the terms in days and the cautions, and that it is the freely readable one."@en .

pmw:SummaPerfectionisMagisterii a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Summa perfectionis magisterii"@en, "Summa perfectionis magisterii"@de ;
    skos:note "Not named by the registration and added to the corpus here, unopened, because the run that read the Turba found the gap this work fills. The Turba states two acts of assay and both judge its own operation; it never states a way of knowing whether a material is what it is called. The Summa is reported to carry a closed catalogue of assays of the metal — cupellation, cementation, ignition, fusion, exposure to sharp vapours, quenching, mixture with sulphur, calcination and reduction, and the taking up of quicksilver — announced as a list and then worked through one by one. Whether that is at the wording decides a question this holding is carrying, so the work belongs in the corpus even though nobody here has opened it."@en ;
    skos:note "Its ascription is disputed and the dispute matters for the contact route. The work circulated as Geber's, that is as Jabir ibn Hayyan's; the received position since Newman's edition of 1991 is that it is by Paul of Taranto, a Latin Franciscan of the late thirteenth century, and the minority position of al-Hassan holds to an Arabic original. If the received position is right, the assay catalogue is a Latin addition to the transmission and not something the Latin took over, which would make it a reworking in the sense of pm:Reworking rather than a copy. Nothing of that is claimed here."@en .

#
# The regimen and what it is said to bring about
#

pmp:TurbaRegimenUniusRei a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "The ruling of the one matter through black, white and red"@en, "Die Führung der einen Materie durch Schwarz, Weiß und Rot"@de ;
    skos:definition "The single operation the work prescribes under many names. The operator takes what the work calls our copper together with the permanent water, closes the vessel, and cooks, imbues, washes and grinds by turns over a fire that must be gentle at first and stronger later, until the matter turns black, then white, then red. The work insists that the many names — salting, sublimating, washing, pounding, whitening, turning into rust, the confection of Ethel — all name this one operation, that nothing foreign is added to it at any point, and that a single decoction and a single grinding would suffice if the operator knew it."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as one practice and not as a series, because the work says so itself at Dictum 53 and again at Dictum 70: there are many names and one regimen, the master began with one and finished one. A holding that entered a node for each named operation would be reporting the vocabulary of the concealment rather than the procedure."@en ;
    skos:note "The circularity SPEC §11 forbids is avoided here by what the practice is said to produce: not our copper, which is the material it starts from, but a tincture that colours a body it is imposed on. What the operation makes is claimed to be different in kind from what it works with, and that is what makes the claim enterable at all."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 9 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 53 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 70 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

pmc:TurbaTincturaInvariabilis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "The invariable tincture that colours a body and does not flee the fire"@en, "Die unveränderliche Tinktur, die einen Körper färbt und das Feuer nicht flieht"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the work is for. A tincture is reached whose colour does not vary, does not flee from the fire and cannot be rubbed out; imposed on the coins of the vulgar it turns them into gold. The work states of it that it colours nothing until it has itself been coloured, that a dense thing does not tinge a tenuous one, and that the tincture is a soul extracted from a body and then put back into a body."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim as the work makes it has one property that decides how it could ever be examined: the outcome is a metal in somebody's hand, which any bystander can weigh, hammer and look at. It is one of the few capacities in this holding whose success would be readable by a stranger without the operator's report. What the work does not state is any act by which the stranger should read it — see pmp:TurbaMateriaeProbaSearch in examinations/searches-latin-alchemy.ttl."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 9 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 25 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

pmc:TurbaTincturaExRegimine a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "The ruling of the one matter brings forth the invariable tincture"@en, "Die Führung der einen Materie bringt die unveränderliche Tinktur hervor"@de ;
    skos:definition "The work's central claim of effect: the operator who turns the copper white and then red until a tincture results has the tincture, and imposing it on base coin makes gold; and the operator who does not turn it so, in the work's own words, accomplishes nothing."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:TurbaRegimenUniusRei ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:TurbaTincturaInvariabilis ;
    skos:note "The claim is stated in both directions in the same sentence, which is rarer in this holding than the positive half alone. At Dictum 9: unless you turn the copper into white and then again into redness until a tincture results, verily, ye accomplish nothing. The negative half is a condition on the procedure and not a criterion on the outcome, so it does not by itself make the claim testable; what it does is fix the operation so that a bystander could at least tell whether it had been carried out."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 9 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

pmp:TurbaDomusCircaArborem a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "The house built round the tree, with a man of a hundred years shut inside"@en, "Das um den Baum gebaute Haus, mit einem Hundertjährigen darin verschlossen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The operation reported at Dictum 58 in the words of a man who said he had found the tree and eaten of its fruit. Take that tree and build a house wholly surrounding it, circular, dark, encircled by dew; place in it a man of a hundred years; shut and secure the door so that neither dust nor wind reaches them; and after a hundred and eighty days send them away to their homes. The man is not to cease eating of the fruit until the number of the days is complete."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a practice although it is framed as a report, because the passage gives an apparatus, a tenant, a closure condition and a term in days, which is what a prescription consists of. The frame matters and is recorded at the yielding node: the speaker did not do it, he asked somebody who said he had."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 58 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

pmc:TurbaSenexIuvenescens a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "The old man made young, the father made into the son"@en, "Der Greis, der jung wird, und der Vater, der zum Sohn wird"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity claimed of the fruit of that tree: the old man shall become young, the soul of the old man is transformed into a juvenile body, and the father is made into the son. Whoever eats of the fruit shall hunger nevermore."@en ;
    skos:note "Read as what it is and not as an allegory of the matter, because the passage gives a number of days and a closed room and the work elsewhere gives numbers of days for the matter as well; nothing in the dictum marks this one as figurative that does not equally mark the others. Whether the compiler meant it so is a question about the compiler. What the graph holds is that the work states it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 58 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

pmc:TurbaIuvenescentiaExArbore a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "A hundred and eighty days of the fruit make the old man young"@en, "Hundertachtzig Tage der Frucht machen den Greis jung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim of effect at Dictum 58: the man of a hundred years shut in the dark house round the tree, eating of its fruit for a hundred and eighty days, becomes young."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:TurbaDomusCircaArborem ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:TurbaSenexIuvenescens ;
    skos:note "The chain of report is three deep and the work states all three links, which is why the node is enterable. Balgus says that a certain person who had followed science notified to him after what manner he discovered the tree, extracted the fruit and ate of it; Balgus then asked him how the tree is nourished until it fructifies, and the answer is the prescription. So the work reports a conversation about somebody else's performance. Nothing in it is offered as the speaker's own."@en ;
    skos:note "It is one of the two claims in the work with a term that falls due: a hundred and eighty days, and at the end of them either the old man is young or he is not. No clause anywhere in the dictum provides for the second case. That silence is what makes the passage worth the node, and it is the reason this file's search for a stated term of abandonment was carried."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 58 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

pmc:TurbaAquaVitae a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "The water of life, in which whatever is mixed first dies and then becomes young"@en, "Das Wasser des Lebens, worin was gemischt wird erst stirbt und dann jung wird"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim Theophilus makes at Dictum 59 in continuation of the tree: the philosophers spoke the truth when they named a water of life, for whatsoever is mixed with that water first dies, then lives and becomes young."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered beside the tree because the two dicta stand together and the second is offered as the explanation of the first. The speaker breaks off immediately afterwards with the sentence that silence is better than this illumination, which is the work's ordinary way of closing a passage and is recorded here because it is the point at which the sequence of death and rejuvenation stops being expanded."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 59 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

#
# What must be there first, with what the work says follows from its absence
#

pmc:TurbaAquaPermanensNecessaria a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "Without the permanent water nobody may enter the art"@en, "Ohne das beständige Wasser darf niemand in die Kunst eintreten"@de ;
    skos:definition "The condition Dardaris states at Dictum 19 and Bonellus repeats at Dictum 37. One who is introduced to this art is to attempt nothing until he is familiar with the power of the permanent water; he who does not understand the permanent water and its indispensable regimen may not enter into this art; and the whole work and regimen does not take place except by water."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:TurbaRegimenUniusRei ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:TurbaAquaPermanens ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Nothing is effected without the permanent water — the work's own words at Dictum 19 — and at Dictum 37 what the operator looks for shall in no wise take place."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 19 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 37 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

pmc:TurbaAquaPermanens a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "The permanent water, called gum, sharp vinegar and spiritual blood"@en, "Das beständige Wasser, genannt Gummi, scharfer Essig und geistliches Blut"@de ;
    skos:definition "The one substance the work makes every operation depend on. It is described as a water that does not evaporate, whose force is a spiritual blood, which turns a body into spirit when ground with it; the work identifies it in turn with the gum, with a very sharp vinegar that makes gold into sheer spirit, with the water of our sea and with the water of sulphur, and states that without it neither whiteness nor blackness nor redness nor rust can be made."@en ;
    skos:note "The identification is the work's own and is made explicitly at four places, which is why one node carries the several names rather than several nodes carrying one name each. At Dictum 18 the gum is none other than permanent water; at Dictum 15 the nature is a very sharp vinegar; at Dictum 21 the despised stone is a permanent water; at Dictum 51 the water of our copper is the fiery venom. What the substance is in a laboratory is not stated anywhere and is not guessed at here."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 15 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 18 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 19 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

pmc:TurbaAlbedoAnteRubedinem a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "Unless ye whiten ye cannot make red"@en, "Wenn ihr nicht weiß machst, könnt ihr nicht rot machen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The order Zimon states at Dictum 17 and Lucas repeats at Dictum 12: unless ye whiten, ye cannot make red; the beginning of the whole work is the whitening, to which the redness succeeds. At Dictum 12 the operator is told that if the fire blazes and the matter reddens early this profits him nothing, because at the beginning of the ruling he requires the white."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:TurbaRubedo ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:TurbaAlbedo ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The redness reached before the whiteness profits nothing and the operator has to go back: the work says at Dictum 12 that in that case it becomes red prematurely, and this will profit you nothing, because in the beginning of the ruling you require the white."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the firmest prerequisite in the work and the only one whose violation has a perceptible mark: a red matter at a point where the work requires a white one. It is therefore the single place where the work's order could be checked from outside, and it is the reason the colour sequence is held in this file as nodes of its own rather than folded into the regimen."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 12 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 17 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 15 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

pmc:TurbaNigredo a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "The blackness, which is called the key of the work"@en, "Die Schwärze, die der Schlüssel des Werkes genannt wird"@de ;
    skos:definition "The first of the three states of the matter. The work has the operator cook until the matter is entirely black and says that when it is, whiteness is hidden in the belly of that blackness; Florus calls the blackening sulphur the key of the work at Dictum 69, and Agadimon at Dictum 63 states flatly that the things mixed and cooked once will show the prescribed blackness, that is, that they all become black."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 63 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 69 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

pmc:TurbaAlbedo a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "The whiteness, extracted most subtly from the blackness"@en, "Die Weiße, aufs Feinste aus der Schwärze gezogen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The second state. The whiteness hidden in the belly of the blackness is to be extracted most subtly, put in a vessel with its instruments and cooked gently until it becomes completely white; the work says at Dictum 67 that to whiten is to burn, and at Dictum 52 that sulphur alone whitens copper and cannot whiten it until it has itself been whitened in the work."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 69 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 52 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

pmc:TurbaRubedo a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "The redness, of a Tyrian colour, with which nothing can compare"@en, "Die Röte von tyrischer Farbe, mit der sich nichts vergleichen lässt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The third and last state. When the whiteness appears and flows over all, the work says the operator is to be certain that redness is hid in that whiteness, and to cook until the whole becomes a most deep red with which nothing can compare; the finished redness is called the Tyrian colour, and at Dictum 67 making red is called making alive."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 69 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 67 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

pmc:TurbaAcetumAnteIgnem a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "The body set over the fire without the vinegar is burnt and corrupted"@en, "Der Körper ohne Essig über das Feuer gesetzt verbrennt und verdirbt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The condition Frictes states at Dictum 15: know that if ye place the body over the fire without vinegar, it will be burnt and corrupted; and know further that the first humour is cold, so be careful of the fire, which is inimical to cold."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:TurbaRegimenUniusRei ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:TurbaAquaPermanens ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The body is burnt and corrupted."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the one prerequisite in the work whose stated consequence is a change in the matter that anybody present would see, rather than a failure to arrive at the result. It is therefore the kind of claim the work could be wrong about in a way that shows, and it is entered separately from the general dependence on the permanent water for that reason."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 15 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

pmc:TurbaSolEtUmbraNecessaria a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "No tingeing venom without the Sun and its shadow"@en, "Kein färbendes Gift ohne die Sonne und ihren Schatten"@de ;
    skos:definition "The condition Pandolfus states at Dictum 21: no body is more precious or purer than the Sun, and no tingeing venom is generated without the Sun and its shadow. He, therefore, who attempts to make the venom of the philosophers without these already errs, and has fallen into that pit wherein his sadness remains; but he who has tinged the venom out of the Sun and its shadow has arrived at the highest arcanum."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:TurbaTincturaInvariabilis ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:TurbaAquaPermanens ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The operator already errs and has fallen into the pit wherein his sadness remains. The consequence is stated as a condition of the man and not of the matter, which is the pattern this file records at pmc:TurbaCulpaInOperante."@en ;
    skos:note "The prior step is entered as the permanent water because the work identifies the Sun's moisture with it; what the Sun and its shadow are is not stated, and the translator's note that the shadow of copper means a protoxide is his and not the work's. What the node claims is the shape of the condition and not the identity of the substances."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 21 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

pmc:TurbaCorpusSolvendum a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "Unless the body is broken up into an impalpable spirit the labour is in vain"@en, "Wird der Körper nicht zu einem untastbaren Geist zerlegt, ist die Arbeit umsonst"@de ;
    skos:definition "The condition stated at Dictum 35 and again at Dictum 71. Unless ye disintegrate this body, imbue and pound both cautiously and diligently until ye extract it from its grossness and turn it into a tenuous and impalpable spirit, ye have your labour in vain; and unless the body be withered up and so destroyed that it dies, and unless ye extract from it its soul, which is a tingeing spirit, ye are unable to tinge a body therewith. The work gives the formula in the received form: except ye turn bodies into not-bodies, and incorporeal things into bodies, ye have not yet discovered the rule of operation."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:TurbaTincturaInvariabilis ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:TurbaRegimenUniusRei ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The labour is in vain and the operator is unable to tinge a body."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 35 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 71 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

pmc:TurbaNaturaeVeritatisAnteOpus a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "Do not approach the work in ignorance of the natures; nothing follows but harm"@en, "Tritt nicht unwissend an das Werk; es folgt nur Schaden"@de ;
    skos:definition "The condition Parmenides sets at Dictum 11. Having noticed the facts in this art, set your hands to the work; if indeed ye know not the natures of truth, do not approach the work, since there will follow nothing but harm, disaster, and sadness. The knowledge required is specified in the same speech: what putrefies the nature of truth and what renews it, what savour it has, what neighbours it has, and how after love enmity and corruption intervene."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:TurbaRegimenUniusRei ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:TurbaTincturaInvariabilis ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Nothing but harm, disaster, and sadness."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered although the prior step is a body of knowledge and not an operation, because the work states the dependence with a consequence and the class takes what the source sets. The prior step is filled with the capacity itself, which is the closest the work comes to naming what is to be known; that is a choice of this file and is stated here so that a later reader can dispute it rather than discover it."@en ;
    skos:note "The stated consequence is harm to the operator and not failure of the work, which puts this node in the small group in this holding where the penalty falls on the person. What the harm consists in is not said."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 11 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

#
# The cautions, nine of them, and one warns against the work's own literature
#

pmc:TurbaCaveNeIgnisArdeat a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "Beware lest the fire blaze up, for then it reddens too soon"@en, "Hütet euch, dass das Feuer auflodert, denn dann wird es zu früh rot"@de ;
    skos:definition "The caution at Dictum 12, given with its consequence: cover the mouth of the vessel securely, and beware lest the fire blaze up, for in this case it becomes red prematurely, and this will profit you nothing, because in the beginning of the ruling you require the white. The same speech adds that the fire is to be gentle in the whitening until coagulation takes place."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:TurbaRegimenUniusRei ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 12 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

pmc:TurbaCaveNeComburatur a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "Take care lest it be burnt, and lest the vinegar turn to smoke and perish"@en, "Sorgt dafür, dass es nicht verbrennt und der Essig nicht zu Rauch wird und vergeht"@de ;
    skos:definition "The cautions Socrates attaches to the long coction at Dictum 16. When the lead becomes dry it drinks up what remains of its humour swiftly, because it is burnt lead — take care, however, lest it be burnt; pound it with the sharpest vinegar and cook till it becomes thick, taking care lest the vinegar be changed into smoke and perish; and when the orpiment is mixed with the lead and cooked for seven days, take care lest the arcanum smoke away."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:TurbaRegimenUniusRei ;
    skos:note "Three cautions in one speech and all three about the same loss: the volatile part leaving the vessel. The work returns to it at Dictum 18, where the operator is told to observe the vessel lest the composition fly and pass off in fumes, and at Dictum 58, where the vessel is to be closed internally and externally with clay. It is the one thing the work warns about more often than it warns about the fire."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 16 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 18 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

pmc:TurbaCaveNeExtrahasFestinanter a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "Beware lest you extract it hastily, for it lingers"@en, "Hütet euch, es eilig herauszuziehen, denn es verweilt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The caution Zenon gives at Dictum 26 when the colours have run through to the purple: when ye are composing the Ixir, beware lest you extract the same hastily, for it lingers. The shorter recension makes the consequence explicit where the longer leaves it at the lingering: beware lest you extract the spirit in haste, for perchance it will perish."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:TurbaRegimenUniusRei ;
    skos:note "The divergence between the recensions is recorded in the definition because it is the difference between a caution with a consequence and one without, and the strength a later reader would assign to the node depends on which recension he holds. Waite's text has the weaker form and his note the stronger."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 26 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension, with the shorter recension in Waite's note)" .

pmc:TurbaCaveNeBalneumNimium a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "Do not kindle the bath excessively, lest they be deprived of sense and motion"@en, "Heizt das Bad nicht übermäßig, damit sie nicht Sinn und Bewegung verlieren"@de ;
    skos:definition "The caution Diomedes gives at Dictum 29, stated of the matter under the figure of a married pair. Place the citrine with his wife after the conjunction into the bath; do not kindle the bath excessively, lest they be deprived of sense and motion; and beware lest ye evaporate them by burning them in too strong a fire. Venerate the king and his wife and do not burn them, since you know not when you may have need of these things."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:TurbaRegimenUniusRei ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 29 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

pmc:TurbaCaveNeIntendasIgnem a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "Beware of intensifying the fire until the elements are conjoined"@en, "Hütet euch, das Feuer zu verstärken, bevor die Elemente verbunden sind"@de ;
    skos:definition "The caution Horfolcus gives at Dictum 65 for the beginning of the mixing: commingle elements which are crude, gentle, sincere and not cooked, over a gentle fire; beware of intensifying the fire until the elements are conjoined, for these should follow one another and be embraced in a complexion, whereby they are gradually burnt until they be desiccated in the said gentle fire. Dictum 61 states the same of the sand: unless ye have patience, ye err in ruling and corrupt the work, and if the composition be ruled more than it should be, its light is taken and extinguished."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:TurbaRegimenUniusRei ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 65 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 61 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

pmc:TurbaCaveVenenaOperantem a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "Beware of separating from the waters, lest the poisons get at you"@en, "Hütet euch vor der Trennung von den Wassern, damit die Gifte nicht an euch gelangen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The one caution in the work whose stated victim is the operator's own body. Bonellus closes Dictum 37 with it: beware, however, of making a separation from the waters lest the poisons get at you, and the body perish with the other things which are in the vessel. The shorter recension reads that the body and soul which are in the vessel shall quickly perish, which removes the operator from the sentence."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:TurbaRegimenUniusRei ;
    skos:note "The divergence decides what the node is about and is therefore in the definition rather than a note. On the longer recension the work warns a man that a vapour can reach him; on the shorter it warns him that his matter can be lost. Waite's text has the first and his note the second, and this holding records both because the count of transmissions that state a bodily danger to the practitioner is small and a mistaken entry would inflate it."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also the work's only physical hazard, and the work states no precaution for it beyond the instruction not to separate: no ventilation, no distance, no covering, no antidote. A transmission that names a poison and prescribes nothing against it is stating a danger rather than managing one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 37 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension, with the shorter recension in Waite's note)" .

pmc:TurbaCaveNeMultiplicetis a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "Beware lest ye multiply things, for the envious multiplied in order to deceive"@en, "Hütet euch, die Dinge zu vervielfachen, denn die Neider haben vervielfacht, um zu täuschen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The work's warning against its own literature, stated by Constans at Dictum 56. Beware, O all ye investigators of this art, lest ye multiply things, for the envious have multiplied and destroyed for you; they have also described various regimens that they might deceive; they have further likened it to the humid with all the humid and the dry with all the dry, by the name of every stone and metal, gall of animals of the sea, the winged things of heaven and reptiles of the earth."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:TurbaRegimenUniusRei ;
    skos:note "This is the caution that gives the work its shape and it is stated a dozen times in different speakers' mouths: the envious wrote obscurely on purpose, the plurality of names is a device against the unworthy, and the reader who follows the names will find nothing. The work therefore instructs its reader to distrust the very corpus it belongs to, and it does so while adding to it. Its practical effect is that no reading of the work can be shown to be wrong from the work's own vocabulary."@en ;
    skos:note "The warning has a second half at Dictum 72 which states the purpose plainly: unless the names were changed, so that the vulgar might be deceived, many would deride our art. So the concealment is not defended as protection of the reader but as protection of the art's standing."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 56 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 72 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

pmc:TurbaCaveNeOstendas a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "Show it not to the evil, and beware of the wicked"@en, "Zeigt es den Bösen nicht, und hütet euch vor den Schlechten"@de ;
    skos:definition "The restriction on passing it on, stated inside the operative speeches. At Dictum 16, after the power of orpiment: do not show these unto the evil, for they will laugh. At Dictum 21, after the perfection of the work: I conjure you by God and your sure Master that you show not this great arcanum, and beware of the wicked. At Dictum 15 the ground is given: I swear to you that if kings were familiar with it, none of us would ever attain this thing."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:TurbaRegimenUniusRei ;
    skos:note "Two of the three grounds the work gives for silence are about the holders and not about the recipients: that the vulgar would laugh, and that kings would take the thing away. Only the third — at Dictum 64, that God has concealed this from the crowd lest the world should be devastated — states a harm to anybody else."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 16 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 21 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 64 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

#
# The two acts of assay, the one term that falls due, and the signs of success
#

pmc:TurbaProbaImpalpabilis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "The powder taken in the hand, impalpable as water, else cook again"@en, "Das Pulver in die Hand genommen, untastbar wie Wasser, sonst nochmals kochen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The work's plainest act of assay, stated by Custos at Dictum 28. When the water has entered the body it turns it first into earth and next into powder; to test the perfection of which take in the hand, and if ye find it impalpable as water, it is then most excellent; otherwise, repeat the cooking until it is brought to the required condition."@en ;
    skos:note "It has everything an assay needs and one thing besides. The act is definite — take the powder in the hand; the criterion is perceptible and needs no instrument; and the negative outcome is named rather than passed over. The one thing besides is what the negative outcome licenses: not a verdict on the operation but a repetition of it, until the outcome arrives. So the act can establish that this batch is not yet finished and cannot establish that the operation does not work."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a claim of the work and not as a protocol, because nobody in this holding has performed it. It is a witness for pmc:TheTestOnTheIngredient in generalizations/the-test-on-the-ingredient.ttl, on the limb about a handbook that assays its own operation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 28 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

pmc:TurbaProbaPulveris a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "Look and see whether it has become a powder; if not, a stronger fire"@en, "Sieh nach, ob es Pulver geworden ist; wenn nicht, ein stärkeres Feuer"@de ;
    skos:definition "The work's second act of assay, at Dictum 13, in the same form as the first. Take that gum which is from the scoriae, mix with cinder of calx and with the faeces, moistening with permanent water; then look and see whether it has become a powder, but if not, roast in a fire stronger than the first fire, until it be pounded."@en ;
    skos:note "The same arrangement as at Dictum 28 with a different remedy: where the first prescribes more of the same, this one prescribes a hotter fire. Both are repetitions of the operation and neither leaves the failing outcome standing. Recorded separately because a count of how a corpus handles its own failing assays needs the cases separately, and these are the only two the work has."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 13 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

pmc:TurbaNigredoNonUltraQuadraginta a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "The blackness must not stay longer than forty days"@en, "Die Schwärze darf nicht länger als vierzig Tage bleiben"@de ;
    skos:definition "The one term in the work whose overrun would be a failure the operator could date. Pandolfus at Dictum 21: rule it thus for the white until it becomes Kuhul, closed up with blackness, which blackness see that it does not remain longer than forty days. The same speech then has the four cooked permanently for forty or forty-two days, after which God will show the operator the principle of the stone."@en ;
    skos:note "What makes the sentence unusual in this holding is that it is an upper bound on a state and not a duration of an operation. Every other number in the work says how long to cook; this one says how long the matter is allowed to stay as it is. An operator whose matter is still black on the forty-first day has a fact about his own batch, dated, and needing nobody's report but his own."@en ;
    skos:note "And what the work does with that fact is nothing. No sentence anywhere in the seventy-two dicta says what the operator is to conclude, do or abandon when the forty days pass and the blackness stands. The bound is stated and left, which is why the search for a term of abandonment was carried over the whole work and is recorded as an exhibited gap at pmp:TurbaTerminusDesistendiSearch."@en ;
    skos:note "The Latin of the passage, as Waite gives it in his note, reads that the pure blackness lasts only forty days, which is an assertion about what happens rather than an instruction about what is allowed. His English makes it an instruction. Which of the two the work says is a question for the Latin and it is left open here; the node claims the bound and not its grammatical mood."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 21 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

pmc:TurbaSignumPrimaeDecoctionis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "The sign of the goodness of the first decoction is the extraction of its redness"@en, "Das Zeichen der Güte der ersten Kochung ist das Ziehen ihrer Röte"@de ;
    skos:definition "The work's stated sign of success, given by Florus at Dictum 69. The sign of the goodness of the first decoction is the extraction of its redness. When ye see that the matter is entirely black, know that whiteness has been hidden in the belly of that blackness; and when ye shall perceive that whiteness appear and flowing over all, be certain that redness is hid in that whiteness."@en ;
    skos:note "Held separately from the two acts of assay, and separately counted, for the reason SPEC §15 gives: a sign is announced to the operator in advance, so he reports it, and the base rate is unknown. The difference from the assays above is exact. At Dictum 28 the operator does something and reads an outcome that could have come out otherwise; here he watches for a colour he has been told to expect, and the instruction that accompanies it is to be certain that the next colour is already hidden in the one he sees."@en ;
    skos:note "The clause that removes the sign's force stands in the same sentence as the sign. Told that whiteness is hidden in the blackness and redness in the whiteness, an operator who sees only blackness has been given the reading that the whiteness is there and not yet out. There is no state of the matter that the sentence would count as the absence of progress."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 69 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

pmc:TurbaRubedoExtrahenda a pm:Disputing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the hidden redness is to be extracted, disputed between the two recensions"@en, "Ob die verborgene Röte zu ziehen ist, zwischen den beiden Rezensionen bestritten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Two opposite instructions at the same place. Of the redness hidden in the whiteness, the longer recension says that it does not behove the operator to extract it but rather to cook it until the whole becomes a most deep red; the shorter recension says that it does behove him to extract it. The dispute is between witnesses of one work and not between parties, and it governs what the operator does at the last stage."@en ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:TurbaSignumPrimaeDecoctionis ;
    skos:note "Entered as a dispute rather than smoothed into the node above because SPEC §10 says an averaged node reports a text no witness gives, and here the average would be no instruction at all. Which recension is older is not decided here. Waite calls the shorter one the clearer and the less corrupt of the two and takes the longer for his text, so his own judgement and his own practice point different ways at exactly this place."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the sharpest instance in the work of a question a later run can settle cheaply and this run did not: the 1610 Basel collection prints both recensions in one volume, so the divergence is a page turn away for anybody who reads the Latin. It is entered as a dispute and not as a debt because the divergence itself is the finding, and it survives whatever the Latin says about which reading is original."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 69 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 69 (trans. Waite 1896, shorter recension as given in Waite's note)" .

pmc:TurbaLapisCoruscans a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "Ruled until it coruscates like marble in its splendour"@en, "Geführt, bis es wie Marmor in seinem Glanz blitzt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The second sign the work announces, given three times in the same words. Gregorius at Dictum 27: the envious have called the venerable stone Efflucidinus and have ordered it to be ruled until it coruscates like marble in its splendour; the copper commingled with vinegar and ruled until it becomes water, then congealed, remains a coruscating stone with a brilliancy like marble, and when ye see it thus it is the most great arcanum. Dictum 12 has the flower of white salt boiled seven days until it becomes like gleaming marble, and Dictum 37 has the stone ruled until it becomes like marble in its splendour."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted with the signs and not with the assays, on the same ground as pmc:TurbaSignumPrimaeDecoctionis. The comparison is to a material anybody could hold up beside the vessel, which makes it the most nearly public sign in the work; what is missing is any statement of what a matter that never comes to that brilliance would establish."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 27 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 12 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 37 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

pmc:TurbaDeusOstendetPrincipium a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "After these days God will show you the principle of this stone"@en, "Nach diesen Tagen wird Gott dir den Anfang dieses Steins zeigen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The third of the work's announced signs, and the one that hands the outcome to somebody outside the procedure. Pandolfus at Dictum 21: cook the four permanently for forty or forty-two days; after these days God will show you the principle of this stone, which is the stone Atitos, of which favoured sight of God there are many accounts. The same speaker has the operator pray God that He may grant him the sight of this very great stone before the cooking begins."@en ;
    skos:note "The translator's own note says the authenticity of the name Atitos is extremely doubtful and that it is probably a misreading, so nothing here turns on the name. What the node claims is the form of the sentence: a count of days, and then a sight that God gives. An operator at whose forty-second day nothing has been shown has been told in advance that the showing was a favour and not a consequence."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the clause the work reaches for wherever a term falls due, and it is not confined to one speaker: by the will of God at Dictum 19, God favouring at Dictum 65, God will accomplish the arcanum ye seek at Dictum 54, until God shall extract the colours for us at Dictum 27, according to the Divine pleasure at Dictum 32. Five speakers, one device."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 21 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 54 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

#
# Where the work puts a run that produced nothing. Four places, four speakers
#

pmc:TurbaCulpaInOperante a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "If ye err, blame no one save yourselves"@en, "Wenn ihr irrt, klagt niemanden an als euch selbst"@de ;
    skos:definition "The work's standing account of a failure, stated by two speakers in the same terms. Zimon at Dictum 17, having given the rule of the four seasons: having, therefore, given this example, rule the tingeing natures, but if ye err, blame no one save yourselves. Diomedes at Dictum 29, closing his speech: happy are ye, if ye understand, but if not, I have still performed my duty, and that briefly, so that if ye remain ignorant, it is God who hath concealed the truth from you — blame not, therefore, the Wise, but yourselves, for if God knew that ye possessed a faithful mind, most certainly he would reveal unto you the truth."@en ;
    skos:note "The second of the two sentences is the complete device. It states in one breath that the failure is the reader's, that the cause is God's concealment, and that the concealment is warranted by a defect in the reader's mind. Each of the three would account for a run that produced nothing; together they leave no outcome that could be charged to the instructions."@en ;
    skos:note "A witness for pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation in generalizations/no-failed-run-counts.ttl, on the limb that locates the condition in the operator."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 17 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 29 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

pmc:TurbaExemplumRelinquentis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "The man who took the slow redness for a mistake and gave up the work"@en, "Der Mann, der die ausbleibende Röte für einen Fehler nahm und das Werk aufgab"@de ;
    skos:definition "The case Gregorius reports at Dictum 27, immediately after saying that an operator who rules ignorantly will see none of the colours. I knew a certain person who commenced this work, and operated the natures of truth, who, when the redness was somewhat slow in appearing, imagined that he had made a mistake, and so relinquished the work. The sentence that follows tells the reader what to do instead: observe how ye make the conjunction, for the redness does not delay in coming."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the most exact instance in the holding of a transmission instructing its reader how to read the absence of its own announced sign. The sign is the redness; the operator did not get it when he expected it; and the work records his inference that something had gone wrong as the error, not the missing redness. After this passage no delay can be evidence, because delay has been named in advance as the thing a man mistakes for evidence."@en ;
    skos:note "It is not a commentator's gloss and not a critic's observation. It stands in an operative speech, between the instruction to rule the stone until it reddens and the instruction about the weight, and it is told as the speaker's own acquaintance."@en ;
    skos:note "A witness for pmc:TheRuleAgainstReportingTheAbsence in generalizations/the-rule-against-reporting-the-absence.ttl, on the third of the three forms that statement names: the doubt is read as a fault in the man rather than as information about the procedure. What is unusual here is that the consequence attaches to acting on the doubt rather than to uttering it, and that the man's loss — he gave up a work that was about to succeed — is supplied by the transmission rather than observed by anybody."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 27 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

pmc:TurbaFestinantiaCausaDefectus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "He knew the elements as well as I and failed of haste, sadness and eagerness"@en, "Er kannte die Elemente so gut wie ich und scheiterte an Eile, Trauer und Begehren"@de ;
    skos:definition "The second case, reported by Bacsen at Dictum 39. I once met with a person who was as well acquainted with the elements as I myself, but when he proceeded to rule this disposition, he attained not to the joy thereof by reason of his sadness and ignorance in ruling, and excessive eagerness, desire, and haste concerning the purpose. The speech puts the general rule beside it: ye can reach no useful result without a patient, laborious and solicitous soul, persevering courage and continuous regimen; he who desires to learn over speedily must not have recourse to our books."@en ;
    skos:note "The case is constructed so that knowledge is explicitly not the variable: the man knew the elements as well as the speaker. What is left to carry the failure is four states of his soul, none of which anybody could have established before the attempt and all four of which anybody could find afterwards. It is the same displacement as pmc:TurbaCulpaInOperante with the knowledge condition deliberately closed off."@en ;
    skos:note "The same speech states the other half of the arrangement, and it is the sentence that makes a term impossible: the truth is not to be discerned except after error, and nothing creates greater pain at heart than error in this art, while each imagines that he has almost the whole world and yet finds nothing in his hands. Error is thereby made a station on the way rather than an outcome. See pmc:TurbaVeritasPostErrorem."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 39 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

pmc:TurbaVeritasPostErrorem a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    rdfs:label "The truth is not discerned except after error"@en, "Die Wahrheit wird nur nach dem Irrtum erkannt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim Bacsen states at Dictum 39, in the middle of the longest caution in the work: the philosophers have plainly stated that the truth is not to be discerned except after error, and nothing creates greater pain at heart than error in this art. He who plants trees does not look for fruit save in due season; how then should ye desire to attain this offering when ye have read but a single book, or have adventured only the first regimen?"@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a claim of its own and not as part of the case beside it, because it does something the other three accounts do not: it makes a failed attempt a required step. The other three say that a particular failure was the operator's fault; this one says that failure in general precedes the truth. An operator who accepts it has been given a reading not just of his last attempt but of any number of them."@en ;
    skos:note "It is the nearest thing in the work to a statement about how many attempts are to be expected, and it names no number. The work's day-counts are all counts of cooking within one attempt; the count of attempts is never given, and the sentence above says why it could not be."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 39 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" .

#
# Where the work's operation lies on the axes
#

pmc:TurbaRegimenSensoryAnchoring a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "The ruling of the one matter, situated on sensory anchoring"@en, "Die Führung der einen Materie, auf der Sinnesbindung verortet"@de ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:TurbaRegimenUniusRei ;
    pm:axisValue pm:outwardWaking ;
    pm:situationGround "Every act the work prescribes is performed with the eyes on the vessel: look and see whether it has become a powder, take the powder in the hand, observe the vessel lest the composition fly, open the vessel and you will find, see that the blackness does not remain longer than forty days. Nothing in the seventy-two dicta asks the operator to withdraw attention from the sense channel, and the one condition it does place on his inner state — patience, freedom from haste, a faithful mind — is a condition on his conduct over months and not a state he enters. The value is therefore the ordinary waking one, and the placement is a reading of the work and not a term the work uses."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference .

pmc:TurbaRegimenAgencyHolding a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "The ruling of the one matter, situated on who holds the agency"@en, "Die Führung der einen Materie, auf der Handlungsträgerschaft verortet"@de ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:TurbaRegimenUniusRei ;
    pm:axisValue pm:ownAgency ;
    pm:situationGround "The placement is disputable and the ground says why it was made. Every operation in the work is the operator's: he mixes, closes, cooks, pounds, imbues, washes and opens, and no passage has another party act in his place. Against that, the work makes the result a gift at five separate places — God will show you the principle, by the will of God, God favouring, God will accomplish the arcanum, according to the Divine pleasure — and at Dictum 29 it makes a failure God's concealment. Those clauses condition the outcome rather than transfer the act, which is why the value is own agency; a reader who holds that a transmission whose outcome is given cedes agency would place it at the other value, and this file states the alternative rather than hiding it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference .
